          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I get migraines about eight times a month. Is that the level where guidance describes prevention, and what lifestyle changes are described?",
          "answer": "Experiencing migraines eight times a month typically warrants a discussion about preventive treatment. Guidance suggests that preventive treatment may be offered if you have four or more migraine days per month with some disability, or fewer attacks if they are severe, or if acute treatments are not effective or suitable (National headache society guidance, guideline-migraine-acute-and-preventive-treatment).\n\nLifestyle changes commonly recommended for migraine prevention often involve addressing contributing factors. This includes maintaining regular sleep patterns, ensuring meal regularity, and avoiding overuse of acute migraine medication (General clinical reference, condition-migraine). Sudden changes in daily routine, such as sleep patterns, are often associated with migraine attacks (General clinical reference and headache medicine educational summary, lifestyle-migraine-sleep).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
